   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_F7qwBTnK5nJdC2dJ",
   "name": "Percentage Uncertainty in Single Readings",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Percentage Uncertainty in Single Readings"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Percentage uncertainty for a single reading (measured value) is defined by the equation:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**Percentage uncertainty = **$\\frac{uncertainty}{measuredvalue}$**× 100 %**"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A student measures the potential difference on an analogue voltmeter with resolution 0.2 V as 15 V.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the student's reading of the potential difference.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Step 1: Determine the absolute uncertainty**\n\n- The absolute uncertainty of the reading is **half** the resolution of the analogue voltmeter\n\n$\\frac{0.2}{2}$= 0.1 V\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the percentage uncertainty**\n\n$\\frac{0.1}{15}$× 100 % = 0.67 %"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "Always make sure your percentage uncertainty is at least one **significant figure** smaller than the measurement."
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_NbkRWTwx6gM34tKJ",
   "name": "Percentage Uncertainty in Multiple Readings",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Percentage Uncertainty in Multiple Readings"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The percentage uncertainty in measurements from **multiple **readings (e.g., repeat readings) use **half** the **range** of the readings\n\n  - The range of the readings is the **difference **between the highest and lowest reading"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A student achieves the following results in their experiment for the angular frequency, *ω.*\n\n0.154, 0.153, 0.159, 0.147, 0.152\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the mean value of *ω.*\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the mean** **value **\n\nmean *ω = *$\\frac{0.154+0.153+0.159+0.147+0.152}{5}$*= *0.153 rad s<sup>–1</sup>\n\n**Step 2: Calculate half the range (this is the uncertainty for multiple readings)**\n\n$\\frac{1}{2}$× (0.159 – 0.147) = 0.006 rad s<sup>–1a</sup>\n\n**Step 3: Calculate percentage uncertainty**\n\n$\\frac{uncertainty}{measuredvalue}$ × 100 % = $\\frac{\\pmhalftherange}{mean}$ × 100 %\n\n$\\frac{0.006}{0.153}$× 100 % = 3.92 %"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "Remember that percentage uncertainties have **no** units, only the % sign!\n\nYou will not be expected to compound percentage uncertainties for AS."
  }
 ]
}